---
# `name` is how the agent refers to this skill internally.
# Keep it short, lowercase, hyphenated — it should match the folder name.
name: example

# `description` is the ONLY thing the agent sees up front (in its system prompt)
# when deciding whether this skill is relevant. Write it like a trigger: say
# plainly WHAT the skill does and WHEN to reach for it. Vague descriptions never
# fire; specific ones fire reliably.
description: >-
  Compose a polished, structured release announcement for the Discord server.
  Use whenever someone asks to "write release notes", "announce a new version",
  or turn a changelog into a user-facing summary.
---

# Release Announcement Skill

Everything below the frontmatter is the full instruction body. The agent loads
it on demand (via `get_skill_instructions`) only after the description above
convinces it the skill is relevant — so this section can be as long as you need
without bloating every conversation.

When this skill is loaded, turn a raw list of changes into a friendly Discord
announcement:

1. Open with a one-line summary of the release and its version number.
2. Group changes under **✨ New**, **🛠 Improved**, and **🐛 Fixed** headings.
   Omit any heading that has no items.
3. Write each item in plain, user-facing language — no internal jargon, no
   commit hashes.
4. End with a short call to action (e.g. "Update with `docker compose pull`").
5. Keep the whole message under ~1500 characters so it fits one Discord message.

## Optional extras

A skill is just a folder. Beyond this `SKILL.md` you can add:

- `references/` — longer supporting docs (style guides, tone examples). The
  agent reads them on demand with `get_skill_reference(skill_name, path)`.
- `scripts/` — runnable helper scripts the agent can read or execute with
  `get_skill_script(skill_name, path)`.

Both are optional; a single `SKILL.md` is a complete skill.
